---
import Presentation from "../../../../components/Presentation.astro";
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";
import { Code as CodeSnippet } from "astro:components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const Figure1 = `let name = prompt("Pilot name?");
let dest = prompt("Destination?");

console.log("Cleared for " + dest);`;
const Figure2 = `Pilot name? Ada
Destination? Titan
Cleared for Titan`;
const Figure3 = `import { Input } from "@cliffy/prompt";

const name = await Input.prompt({
  message: "Pilot name?"
});

console.log("Welcome aboard, " + name);`;
const Figure4 = `? Pilot name? › Ada
Welcome aboard, Ada`;
const Figure5 = `const dest = await Select.prompt({
  message: "Destination?",
  options: [
    { name: "Titan", value: "titan" },
    { name: "Ganymede", value: "ganymede" },
    { name: "Europa", value: "europa" }
  ]
});

console.log(dest);`;
const Figure6 = `titan`;
const Task1 = `import { Input } from "@cliffy/prompt";
import { bold } from "@std/fmt/colors";

console.log(bold("=== Flight Registration ==="));

const pilot = await Input.prompt({
  message: "Pilot name?"
});

console.log("Welcome aboard, " + pilot);
`;
const Task2 = `import { Input, Select } from "@cliffy/prompt";
import { bold } from "@std/fmt/colors";

console.log(bold("=== Flight Registration ==="));

const pilot = await Input.prompt({
  message: "Pilot name?"
});

const dest = await Select.prompt({
  message: "Destination?",
  options: [
    { name: "Titan", value: "titan" },
    { name: "Ganymede", value: "ganymede" },
    { name: "Europa", value: "europa" }
  ]
});

console.log("Welcome aboard, " + pilot + ". Bound for " + dest + ".");
`;
const Task3 = `import { Input, Select } from "@cliffy/prompt";
import { bold, dim } from "@std/fmt/colors";

console.log(bold("=== Flight Registration ==="));

const pilot = await Input.prompt({
  message: "Pilot name?"
});

const dest = await Select.prompt({
  message: "Destination?",
  options: [
    { name: "Titan", value: "titan" },
    { name: "Ganymede", value: "ganymede" },
    { name: "Europa", value: "europa" }
  ]
});

// Ask for a cargo mass with Input.prompt(), and cast it to a number
// Ask for a class of service with Select.prompt(), at least three options
// Work out a fee: 12 per unit of mass, doubled for premium class
// Print a dim divider, then the fee to two decimal places
`;
const ExampleOutput = `=== Flight Registration ===
? Pilot name? › Ada
? Destination? › Titan
? Cargo mass? › 40
? Class of service? › Premium
---
Ada to titan, 40 units, fee $960.00
`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Library Exploration: @cliffy/prompt</h1>

        <h4>
          Difficulty&nbsp;&nbsp;
          <i class="si-star text-sn-green"></i>
          <i class="si-star text-sn-yellow"></i>
          <i class="si-star text-sn-red"></i>
        </h4>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <!-- Launch / Concept -->
        <section id="launch">
          <Presentation>
            <section>
              <h2>Library Exploration</h2>
              <h3 class="text-sn-theme">@cliffy/prompt</h3>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Key Concepts</h2>
              <p>Prompts That Look Designed</p>
              <p>Picking From a List</p>
              <p>Two Bits of Borrowed Syntax</p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>What You Have</h2>
              <Code
                code={Figure1}
                frame="code"
                title="Demo"
                lang="javascript"
                showLineNumbers={false}
                class="mb-3"
              />
              <Code
                code={Figure2}
                frame="terminal"
                lang="txt"
                title="Demo"
                showLineNumbers={false}
                class="fragment"
              />
              <p class="fragment">
                It works. It also gives the user no structure, no options, and no
                way to know how far through they are.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>What This Gives You</h2>
              <Code
                code={Figure3}
                frame="code"
                title="Demo"
                lang="javascript"
                showLineNumbers={false}
              />
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>What This Gives You</h2>
              <Code
                code={Figure4}
                frame="terminal"
                lang="txt"
                title="Demo"
                showLineNumbers={false}
              />
              <p class="fragment">
                The question is drawn for you, and once you answer it, the prompt
                collapses to one tidy line holding your answer. Compare that to
                the plain version above.
              </p>
              <p class="fragment">
                There is no frame around the session &mdash; you build that
                yourself, with a heading. The{" "}
                <CodeSnippet code="@std/fmt/colors" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline /> exploration is where you got the
                tools for it.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Borrowed Syntax, Part One</h2>
              <p>
                <CodeSnippet code="{ message: \"Pilot name?\" }" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline />
              </p>
              <p class="fragment">
                That is an <b class="text-sn-theme">object</b> &mdash; named
                settings inside braces, rather than values in a fixed order. You
                met the same shape in the @cliffy/table exploration with{" "}
                <CodeSnippet code="{ minWidth: 10 }" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline />.
              </p>
              <p class="fragment">
                Copy the shape. It gets taught properly later.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Borrowed Syntax, Part Two</h2>
              <p>
                <CodeSnippet code="await" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline />
              </p>
              <p class="fragment">
                Asking a person a question takes time. The program has to{" "}
                <i>stop and wait</i>.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>Borrowed Syntax, Part Two</h2>
              <p>
                <CodeSnippet code="await" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline /> means "pause here until the answer comes
                back, then carry on."
              </p>
              <p class="fragment">
                Leave it off and your variable holds a{" "}
                <b class="text-sn-theme">Promise</b> &mdash; a placeholder for an
                answer that has not arrived. Your output says{" "}
                <CodeSnippet code="[object Promise]" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline /> and nothing makes sense.
              </p>
              <p class="fragment">
                Every prompt from this library needs it. That is the whole rule.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate data-auto-animate-restart>
              <h2>Picking From a List</h2>
              <Code
                code={Figure5}
                frame="code"
                title="Demo"
                lang="javascript"
                showLineNumbers={false}
                class="mb-3"
              />
              <Code
                code={Figure6}
                frame="terminal"
                lang="txt"
                title="Demo"
                showLineNumbers={false}
                class="fragment"
              />
            </section>

            <section data-auto-animate>
              <h2>Label Versus Value</h2>
              <p>
                The user saw <b>Titan</b>. The variable holds{" "}
                <CodeSnippet code="titan" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline />.
              </p>
              <p class="fragment">
                <b class="text-sn-theme">
                  <CodeSnippet code="name" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline /> is what a human reads.{" "}
                  <CodeSnippet code="value" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline /> is what your code gets.
                </b>{" "}
                They can differ, and usually should.
              </p>
              <p class="fragment">
                Watch the line the prompt leaves behind: it reads{" "}
                <CodeSnippet code="Titan" lang={"txt"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline />, because that is what you
                showed the user. The variable never held it.
              </p>
              <p class="fragment">
                This is why <CodeSnippet code="Select.prompt()" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline /> beats asking someone to type
                a destination: they cannot misspell it, and they cannot invent
                one you did not plan for.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Input Is Still Text</h2>
              <p>
                <CodeSnippet code="Input.prompt()" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline /> hands back a string, exactly like{" "}
                <CodeSnippet code="prompt()" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline /> did.
              </p>
              <p class="fragment">
                A nicer-looking question does not change the type of the answer.
                Cast it with <CodeSnippet code="Number()" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline /> before doing arithmetic, or
                Activity 1.9 happens to you again.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Today's Objectives</h2>
              <ul>
                <li>Framing a session with a styled heading</li>
                <li>Collecting text with a styled prompt</li>
                <li>Offering a fixed set of choices</li>
                <li>Telling a choice's label apart from its value</li>
              </ul>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Key Terms</h2>
              <div class="text-start">
                <dl>
                  <dt>Object</dt>
                  <dd>
                    Named settings grouped in braces, passed as one argument.
                  </dd>
                  <dt>
                    <CodeSnippet code="await" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline />
                  </dt>
                  <dd>Pause until a slow answer arrives, then continue.</dd>
                  <dt>Label</dt>
                  <dd>
                    The text a user sees for a choice. This library spells it
                    <CodeSnippet code="name" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline />.
                  </dd>
                  <dt>Value</dt>
                  <dd>What your program receives when that choice is picked.</dd>
                </dl>
              </div>
            </section>
          </Presentation>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Build -->
        <section id="build">
          <h2>Build</h2>

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">This one is optional and worth extra credit.</b>{" "}
            It is also the hardest of the three explorations, because it borrows
            two pieces of syntax you have not been taught. Copying a shape you do
            not fully understand yet is a normal part of using libraries.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>Do the @cliffy/table exploration first</b> if you have not &mdash; it
            introduces the object syntax more gently.
          </p>

          <p>
            Create a new <b>JavaScript</b> program named{" "}
            <b class="text-sn-theme">le-cliffy-prompt</b>.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">If the very first line errors</b>, or you see
            something about <CodeSnippet code="await" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline /> not being allowed, tell me. That
            is a setup problem, not a mistake you made.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 1: A Framed Question</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Write the code below and run it.</li>
            <li>
              Note the shape of the import: <CodeSnippet code="{ Input }" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline />{" "}
              in braces, because you are taking a named thing out of one library
              rather than the whole thing. There are two import lines here,
              because the heading uses the colors library from the first
              exploration.
            </li>
            <li>
              <b>Then delete the <CodeSnippet code="await" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline /></b> and run it again. Read
              what gets printed. Put it back.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <Code
            code={Task1}
            frame="code"
            lang="javascript"
            title="Library Exploration: @cliffy/prompt"
          />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 2: A Menu</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Add a <CodeSnippet code="Select.prompt()" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline /> with three destinations. Use the arrow
              keys to choose.
            </li>
            <li>
              The last line shows the <i>value</i>, not the label &mdash;
              lowercase <CodeSnippet code="titan" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline />, not <b>Titan</b>. The prompt's own
              line still reads <b>Titan</b> directly above it, which makes the
              difference easy to see. Confirm it for yourself.
            </li>
            <li>
              <b>Worth trying:</b> make one option's value and label identical and
              see whether you prefer it. There is a real argument either way.
            </li>
            <li>
              <b class="text-sn-theme">Note</b>: the green lines with a + sign
              are new code. Don't type the + sign.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <Code
            code={Task2}
            frame="code"
            lang="javascript"
            title="Library Exploration: @cliffy/prompt"
            ins={{ range: "1, 10-17, 19" }}
          />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 3: Plan, Then Price the Flight</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              The comments describe two more prompts and a calculation. Write the
              code under them.
            </li>
            <li>
              <b>Cast the cargo mass.</b> A styled prompt still returns a string,
              and multiplying an uncast one gives you a number while adding gives
              you nonsense &mdash; exactly Activity 1.9.
            </li>
            <li>
              The class of service is a <CodeSnippet code="Select.prompt()" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline />. Compare against
              its <i>value</i> in your conditional, not its label.
            </li>
            <li>
              The fee needs <CodeSnippet code="toFixed(2)" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline />, and remember to format
              only at the moment you display it.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <Code
            code={Task3}
            frame="code"
            lang="javascript"
            title="Library Exploration: @cliffy/prompt"
            ins={{ range: "2, 19-22" }}
          />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Challenge (Optional): Refuse a Bad Answer</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Run your program and type <b>banana</b> for the cargo mass. It is
              accepted, and your fee comes out as{" "}
              <CodeSnippet code="NaN" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline />.
            </li>
            <li>
              Every prompt in this library takes a{" "}
              <CodeSnippet code="validate" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline /> setting that can reject an answer and ask
              again, with a message you write. Use it so a cargo mass has to be a
              number above zero.
            </li>
            <li>
              You will have to read the library's own documentation to do this,
              which is the actual skill being practiced.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Checkpoint -->
        <section id="checkpoint">
          <h2>Checkpoint</h2>

          <p>
            Verify your program works correctly. Your answers will differ from
            the example.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={ExampleOutput}
            frame="terminal"
            lang="txt"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            title="Example Output"
            mark={[
              { label: "The value, not the label", range: "15" },
            ]}
          />

          <p class="mt-3">
            Check the arithmetic by hand: 40 units at 12 each is 480, doubled for
            premium is 960. If your fee comes out as something like{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code="4040404012" lang={"javascript"} theme={"material-theme-ocean"} inline />, you forgot to cast.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Reflection -->
        <section id="reflection">
          <h2>Reflection</h2>

          <p>Answer the following questions before submitting your work.</p>

          <ol>
            <li>
              You removed <code>await</code> in Task 1 and the output changed.
              Describe what you saw, and what you think the program was holding
              in that variable.
            </li>
            <li>
              <code>Select.prompt()</code> stops a user from typing something you did not
              plan for. Give an example from your own programs where that would
              have prevented a bug.
            </li>
            <li>
              This exploration made you copy two pieces of syntax you have not
              been taught. Was that uncomfortable, useful, or both? There is no
              right answer &mdash; but you will do it again in real work.
            </li>
          </ol>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Submit -->
        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>Submit the required files to the appropriate dropbox.</p>

          <h1
            class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
          >
            Exploration Complete
          </h1>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
